## 2. Priority 1 — quick wins

### 2.1 `client_max_body_size` — segmentation saving will fail without it

**CLASS:** FILE CHANGE (bind-mounted config)

**Status:** **NOT configured.** The file currently only *mentions* it in comments.

**Why:** `orthanc-cors-proxy` is stock `nginx:alpine`, whose default body limit is **1 MB**.
OHIF writes segmentations back via STOW-RS `POST`, which exceeds that. Reads are unaffected,
which is why everything else works.

**How** — `orthanc-cors-proxy.conf` is a **single-file bind mount**, so the edit must
preserve the inode (`sed -i` and most editors would silently leave the container serving the
old file):

```bash
cp orthanc-cors-proxy.conf "backup files/orthanc-cors-proxy.conf.bak-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S)"
```

```bash
python3 -c "
p='orthanc-cors-proxy.conf'; t=open(p).read()
a='    listen 80;'
assert t.count(a)==1, 'anchor not found exactly once'
open(p,'w').write(t.replace(a, a+'''

    # OHIF writes segmentations back via STOW-RS POST; the stock nginx 1 MB
    # default rejects them. 0 = no limit.
    client_max_body_size 0;'''))
"
```

```bash
docker exec orthanc-cors-proxy nginx -t && docker exec orthanc-cors-proxy nginx -s reload
```

**Verify:**

```bash
docker exec orthanc-cors-proxy grep -n client_max_body_size /etc/nginx/conf.d/default.conf
```

Then save a segmentation from a workstation — that is the only real test.

**ROLLBACK:** `cat "backup files/<backup>" > orthanc-cors-proxy.conf`, then `nginx -t` and reload.

