### 2.2 Install the hospital CA so browsers stop warning

**CLASS:** FILE CHANGE, requires `sudo`

**Status:** not installed — `curl https://viewer.hospital.lan/` without `-k` returns `000`.

**Why:** certificates are signed by CHU Blida's own CA. Untrusted clients show a warning on
every load. Because OHIF is same-origin, accepting once is *enough to work*, but that trains
clinicians to click through security warnings.

```bash
sudo cp /home/server/certificates/hospitalCA.crt /usr/local/share/ca-certificates/hospitalCA.crt
```

```bash
sudo update-ca-certificates
```

**Verify** — should now return `200` **without** `-k`:

```bash
curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' https://viewer.hospital.lan/
```

Chrome and Firefox keep their **own** trust stores, so the system store alone may not be
enough. If the warning persists, import into the browser's NSS database — note `certutil`
is **not currently installed** on this host:

```bash
sudo apt install libnss3-tools
```

```bash
certutil -d sql:$HOME/.pki/nssdb -A -t "C,," -n "CHU Blida CA" -i /home/server/certificates/hospitalCA.crt
```

`~/.pki/nssdb` exists and is what the `.deb` Chrome (`/usr/bin/google-chrome`) uses.
Snap-packaged Chromium keeps a separate store at `~/snap/chromium/current/.pki/nssdb`,
which is created on first run and does not exist yet — import there too if you use it.

Certificate being installed, for reference:
`CN = CHU Blida Internal CA, O = CHU Blida` — valid until 2036-08-02.

**This must be done on every clinical workstation**, not just the server.

**ROLLBACK:** `sudo rm /usr/local/share/ca-certificates/hospitalCA.crt && sudo update-ca-certificates --fresh`

